    ":question/id": "707504e8-a367-5dc2-a663-371a9dcf7224",
    ":question/math-academy-id": "e-13010",
    ":question/problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n${2u + 9v - 6 = 0; 2u + 6v - 8 = 0$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n{2u + 9v - 6 = 0(1); 2u + 6v - 8 = 0(2)\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $u$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2u+6v-8 &= 0 \\\\ 2u+6v &= 8 \\\\ 2u &= 8 - 6v \\\\ u &= \\dfrac{8-6v}{2} \\\\ u &= 4 - 3v \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $u=4-3v$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $v$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2u+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\ 2(4-3v)+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\ 8-6v+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\ 2+3v &= 0 \\\\ 3v &= -2 \\\\ v &= -\\dfrac{2}{3} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute\n\n$$\nv=-\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\ninto equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $u$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2u+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\ 2u+9\\left(-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right) -6 &= 0 \\\\ 2u-6-6 &= 0 \\\\ 2u-12 &= 0 \\\\ 2u &= 12 \\\\ u &= 6 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=6$ and\n\n$$\nv=-\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\n, which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\left(6,-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)\n$$"
  },
